= Note on the Habits of the Pampas Woodpecker / 潘帕斯啄木鸟习性札记

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("In the last of Mr. Hudson's valuable articles on the ornithology of Buenos Ayres*, he remarks, with respect to my observations on the Colaptes campestris , that it is not possible for a naturalist \"to know much of a species from seeing perhaps one or two individuals in the course of a rapid ride across the Pampas.\" My observations were made in Banda Oriental, on the northern bank of the Plata, where, thirty-seven years ago, this bird was common; and during my successive visits, especially near Maldonado, I repeatedly saw many specimens living on the open and undulating plains, at the distance of many miles from a tree. I was confirmed in my belief, that these birds do not frequent trees, by the beaks of some which I shot being muddy, by their tails being but little abraded, and by their alighting on posts or branches of trees (where such grew) horizontally and crosswise, in the manner of ordinary birds, though, as I have stated, they sometimes alighted vertically. When I wrote these notes, I knew nothing of the works of Azara, who lived for many years in Paraguay, and is generally esteemed as an accurate observer. Now Azara calls this bird the Woodpecker of the plains, and remarks that the name is highly appropriate; for, as he asserts, it never visits woods, or climbs up trees, or searches for insects under the bark†. He describes its manner of feeding on the open ground, and of alighting, sometimes horizontally and sometimes vertically, on trunks, rocks, &c., exactly as I have done. He states that the legs are longer than those of other species of Woodpeckers. The beak, however, is not so straight and strong, nor the tail-feathers so stiff, as in the typical members of the group. Therefore this species appears to have been to a slight extent modified, in accordance with its less arboreal habits. Azara further states that it builds its nest in holes, excavated in old mud walls or in the banks of streams. I may add that the Colaptes pitius , which in Chile represents the Pampas species, likewise frequents dry stony hills, where only a few bushes or trees grow, and may be continually seen feeding on the ground. According to Molina, this Colaptes also builds its nest in holes and banks.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("在哈德逊先生关于布宜诺斯艾利斯鸟类学的宝贵文章中，最后一篇谈到我对潘帕斯啄木鸟（Colaptes campestris）的观察。他说，博物学家不可能‘在匆匆骑马穿越潘帕斯平原时，或许只见到一两只个体，就对一个物种了解很多’。我的观察是在拉普拉塔河北岸的东岸地区作出的；三十七年前，这种鸟在那里很常见。在几次造访期间，尤其是在马尔多纳多附近，我反复见到许多个体生活在开阔而起伏的平原上，离树木有好几英里。我射获的一些鸟，喙上沾着泥，尾部磨损很少；它们落在柱子或树枝上时——在有树的地方——像普通鸟一样，身体横着，方向与枝条交叉，不过我也说过，它们有时会直立着落下。这些情形使我更加相信，它们并不常到树上去。写这些笔记时，我对阿萨拉的著作一无所知；他曾在巴拉圭生活多年，通常被认为是观察准确的人。阿萨拉称这种鸟为平原啄木鸟，并说这个名称十分贴切，因为据他断言，这种鸟从不进入树林，不攀树，也不在树皮下寻找昆虫。他描述它在开阔地面上觅食，以及有时横着、有时直立着落在树干、岩石等处的方式，与我的描述完全一致。他说，它的腿比其他啄木鸟更长。不过，它的喙不如这一类群的典型成员那么直而坚实，尾羽也没有那么硬。因此，这个物种似乎因较少栖居树上的习性而发生了轻微改变。阿萨拉还说，它在旧泥墙或溪岸上挖洞筑巢。我可以补充，在智利与潘帕斯种相对应的 Colaptes pitius，也常出没于干燥多石、只有少量灌木或树木的山丘，随时可以见到它在地上觅食。据莫利纳说，这种啄木鸟也在洞穴和岸壁上筑巢。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("Mr. Hudson, on the other hand, states that near Buenos Ayres, where there are some woods, the Colaptes campestris climbs trees and bores into the bark like other Woodpeckers. He says, \"it is sometimes found several miles distant from any trees. This, however, is rare, and it is on such occasions always apparently on its way to some tree in the distance. It here builds its nest in holes in trees.\" I have not the least doubt that Mr. Hudson's account is perfectly accurate, and that I have committed an error in stating that this species never climbs trees. But is it not possible that this bird may have somewhat different habits in different districts, and that I may not be quite so inaccurate as Mr. Hudson supposes? I cannot doubt, from what I saw in Banda Oriental, that this species there habitually frequents the open plains, and lives exclusively on the food thus obtained. Still less can I doubt the account given by Azara of its general habits of life, and of its manner of nidification. Finally, I trust that Mr. Hudson is mistaken when he says that any one acquainted with the habits of this bird might be induced to believe that I \"had purposely wrested the truth in order to prove\" my theory. He exonerates me from this charge; but I should be loath to think that there are many naturalists who, without any evidence, would accuse a fellow worker of telling a deliberate falsehood to prove his theory.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("另一方面，哈德逊先生说，在有一些树林的布宜诺斯艾利斯附近，潘帕斯啄木鸟像其他啄木鸟一样攀树并啄入树皮。他说：‘有时可以在离任何树木好几英里的地方发现它。不过这种情形很少见，而且此时它总像是在前往远处某棵树的途中。它在这里把巢筑在树洞里。’我丝毫不怀疑哈德逊先生的记述完全准确，也承认我说这个物种从不攀树是错误的。但这种鸟在不同地区是否可能有略微不同的习性，而我的记述是否也不像哈德逊先生所想的那么不准确？根据我在东岸地区亲眼所见，我无法怀疑这个物种在那里惯常出没于开阔平原，并完全依靠在那里获取的食物生活。对于阿萨拉所说的总体生活习性和筑巢方式，我更没有理由怀疑。最后，哈德逊先生说，任何熟悉这种鸟习性的人，都可能因此认为我‘为了证明’自己的理论‘故意歪曲事实’；我希望他在这点上是错的。他本人免除了对我的这项指控；但我不愿相信，有许多博物学家会在毫无证据的情况下，指责同行为了证明自己的理论而故意说谎。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("* P.Z.S. 1870, p. 158.")]

